{"title":"Topicwise Test -1 Part Syllabus GATE 2026 EC Network 1","fileName":"051_Topicwise Test -1 Part Syllabus GATE 2026 EC Network 1.html","branch":"ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING","series":"GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series Electronics Engineering","provider":"MADE EASY","duration":2700,"questions":[{"qnum":1,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>The relative heating effects of two current waves of equal peak value, one rectangular and the other, triangular in shape as shown in figure is,</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/ea1d209d72e41b6b4b93556423b383dcf68e9417.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/3e5f84343b093a138f666f9eef992e94e5738ad2.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 3 : 1</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 3 : 2</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/c1f89e7dd9bc7129e09b9c551fa7bb4761e90613.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/dfb4c8efc1d934439651a60827f5f20fbbbc9380.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n</div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":2,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Two load resistances <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> dissipate the same power when connected individually to a voltage source ‘<em>V<sub>s</sub></em>’ having an internal resistance of <img src=\"/media/12a9ebdf80b7dba25c28ec2a5eb3e435a62538ff.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /><br />\nChoose the correct relation between <img src=\"/media/2cf9cd47f009150c66055e9498fd1be94f87bcc3.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub>.</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/4c9577360abc566c08571c1ebc1d669fb3208b99.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/59c700a66e4f1a084412bba7524a89cb9d79ff1a.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/79dc53d59358e0e30c233f82b512e2fc77a4114d.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/04f9a72188aaa82e27522f48d4298e6c03b9d4f9.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/a5977f341c8b9fe944264fc9100f3eb75b79ad4b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/8bca5fcc82510f20ea710685f9774e332a1dc4d3.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":3,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>A tungsten filament bulb rated at 500 W, 150 V is to be connected in series with a capacitance across 220 V, 50 Hz supply. The value of capacitor such that the voltage and power consumed by the bulb are according to the rating of the bulb is,</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 66 μF</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 33 μF</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 132 μF</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 16.5 μF</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/f44ffe74bdbc50b1e2a4a488de02922381fa2024.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":4,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Which of the following static V-I characteristics represents a Bilateral device?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/29cc4d4988c8d488fa45f86ca6af1ab56ce0782e.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/c2aca96f10b889831c3c97ea0c058e7913c27524.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/9e7005d5be04a8cb3300995800fda994b520b6dd.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/2349f78a97dd58b9ed42a1aa57bb6b11ad4496b5.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nWhen the same relation exists between voltage and current for the current flowing in both directions or in other words, if the VI characteristics curve is similar in opposite quadrants then the element is bidirectional (or) bilateral. Hence, option (d) is correct.</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":5,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>To analyze a complex network which consists of a supernode, which of the following laws are required to write the supernodal equations?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> KVL and Ohm’s law</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> KCL and Ohm’s law</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> KVL, KCL and Ohm’s law</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> KVL and KCL</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nWhen only an ideal voltage source is connected between two non-reference nodes without any impedance, we form a supernode encompassing these two nodes for applying KCL while the voltage between the nodes are related by KVL. Thus, supernodal equations require applications of KCL, KVL and Ohm&rsquo;s law.</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":6,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>The current in the 100 Ω resistor is _________ A. [Rounded off to two decimal places].</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/b47ef54d0920362d392d8bdb92df95919457c679.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"0.48","high":"0.48"},"solution":"<p>0.48 (0.40 - 0.50)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/071dcd648f7aadfd2c71c38f6265d6a65c6feb66.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":7,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>For maximum power transfer, the value of load resistance ‘<em>R<sub>L</sub></em>’ is __________ Ω. [Rounded off to two decimal places].</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/5297b7549601b103d2dcaf25986b04255515a7f3.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"4.67","high":"4.67"},"solution":"<p>4.67 (4.50 - 4.80)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/ba7fe1d4c835e181ca50574a864327a0262f9682.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":8,"type":"NAT","correct":"B","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>The magnitude of Thevenin’s equivalent voltage ‘<em>V</em><sub>Th</sub>’ across the terminals <em>A</em>-<em>B</em> is ________ V.<br />\n[Rounded off to one decimal place]</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/7bda45f182cae1f2a27cf819fcd67d3e3ff96577.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"62.5","high":"62.5"},"solution":"<p>62.5 (62.0 - 63.0)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/fd08dd9fbb398e9a4aeda81b92af3c5ada25503b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/5bbabb37ffdfd9991ba254b4f76bede3aa6a5ea4.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div> </div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":9,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,B","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>When a sinusoidal voltage of 150 V (rms) is applied to a series R-L circuit, it is found that the power dissipation is 1000 W and the current flowing in the circuit is given by <img src=\"/media/0044febaac0cfa0a174bf228b019335c9e14edd7.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /><br />\nWhich of the following statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> The resistance of the circuit is 2.223 Ω.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> The inductance of the circuit is 1.07 H.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> The power factor angle of the circuit is 77.16°.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> Magnitude of total impedance is 5 Ω.</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a, b)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/040876eea40de34e16a119046dff62ee44488e74.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div> </div>\n<div> </div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":10,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>The periodic current <img src=\"/media/df5204dc8af3b6242f9d6de0c119c1d0450d5929.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> in the given figure is applied to a pure resistor of 5 Ω. The power dissipated in the resistor is,</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/7acb5e78adeb33e2f1d61c79583261d3507c4c30.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/426d750634bbcfb68e1696642f30a4b43166cb69.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/d78e41ddcbe25a08fbdce24da6de28d2b431132c.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/94828f8fcc5db001fc57ca02658ac0f4edad8b29.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/6714454d0039348f1a3f3e8a1399119e832db716.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/7fc6bd679c5bd470a63ba8ff22a8a0950bfdfab4.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/59be8460b1540b13b2f0677dfc4a813cd8841436.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":11,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>If a voltage of 200 V applied between terminals <em>A</em> and <em>B</em> produces a current of 25 A for the circuit shown in the figure below, the power factor of the circuit is</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/4832809dabf8e7741a8a5a3fcc25cd4ad10abc60.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 0.707 (lead)</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 0.5 (lag)</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 0.87 (lead)</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 1</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/85b18f68b27570b9a9276e618558fe51c80c9075.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":12,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>The resistive circuit shown in figure (1) is simplified as shown in figure (2). The sum of resistances (<em>a</em> + <em>b</em> + <em>c</em> + <em>d</em> + <em>e</em> + <em>f</em>) is,</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/9225451832cbf378f1df49506e6877b011796fa5.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 57 Ω</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 28.5 Ω</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 14.25 Ω</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 7.125 Ω</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div><img src=\"/media/4d2210fd743542718d3e6ea874d810c00300df7c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<p> </p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":13,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>In the network shown below, the value of <em>V</em><sub>1</sub> so that the current through (1 + <em>j</em>) Ω impedance is zero is,</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/b8ea8865f413fa7e7fa97bd85fa08a9755194bc6.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/58e0e600671155130cbf8ca10a4bf2841186a33d.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/678b79ed7b7dcbf1395646fd9fdf5b592652f003.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/2bb4f755c54055ce5169c85ca4502e16f68b24bf.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/1b4661e322c7f610d5fcb20e228d72a18f8e605e.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/1ab0f2365bb339cb9aa3dfba560981b96e88a0da.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":14,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Two voltage sources have equal emfs ‘<em>E</em>’ and a phase difference ‘θ’. When they are connected in series, the total voltage is 100 V. When one source is reversed, the total voltage is 10 V. The phase angle ‘θ’ is _________ degrees [Rounded off to one decimal place].</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"11.4","high":"11.4"},"solution":"<p>11.4 (11.0 - 11.9)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/e7bf98329a93f5d2a14ef5ae97b4578c36a605c4.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":15,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>If <img src=\"/media/be1053510c998a2e3938e17b0db507a9c399ab20.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> (degrees).<br />\n[Rounded off to one decimal place]</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/6241c7325c9efe08493eaa026eade3b1c5b2874a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"51.2","high":"51.2"},"solution":"<p>51.2 (51.0 - 51.9)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/97492a26e98ce92cbda6910e1785901557a4e1a7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/8757261fcc9225bc2f33e7433ec3b066d711904a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div> </div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":16,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>The square wave voltage seen in figure (a) is applied to the combination of inductors seen in figure (b).</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/984b60a1663406cee571acba9000258ac16a509c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div>The current through the combination of inductors at <em>t</em> = 4 ms is ____________ A.</div></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"10","high":"10"},"solution":"<p>10 (10 - 10)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/09a47d1be4b80beddc0f5e3efe9dbe7f13acf24e.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/62105f3052d4ec968c908225f81de8bc731ccba6.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":17,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,C","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>In the circuit shown below, <img src=\"/media/8a62ec96d39e6689e8cd80d29bc57cf2e7a457ea.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<div> </div>\n<div>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/49ff3ebd54cd600173813a71182e9ad9aa03e6f7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n</div>\n<div>Select the correct options regarding power in the given circuit.</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> The total complex power is 403.04∠+8.07° VA.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> The total real power is 200 W.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> The total reactive power is 56.569 VAR.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> The power factor of the circuit is 0.866.</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a, c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/29c6a65cf06ba4be91a39ae1f93ae1f8e873f144.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/ad8bdc4dfe962cad04f94254e132b1993e214381.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"}],"questionCount":17,"parsedAt":"2026-11-10T19:39:15.571Z"}